      ":question/id": "1f5000c7-50e0-5fb4-a184-91dafeef5f7c",
      ":question/problem": "Which of the numbers below belong to $\\mathbb{Q}_0^{+}$?\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Remember that $\\mathbb{Q}_0^{+}$ is the set of all positive rational numbers and zero.\n\n- $-\\frac{20}{8}=-\\frac{5}{2}$: It is negative, and it is a rational number but not an integer. Therefore, it does *not* belong to $\\mathbb{Q}_0^{+}$.\n\n- $-\\frac{7}{8}$: It is negative, and it is a rational number but not an integer. Therefore, it does *not* belong to $\\mathbb{Q}_0^{+}$.\n\n- $2\\sqrt{7}$: It is positive, and it is an irrational real number. Therefore, it does *not* belong to $\\mathbb{Q}_0^{+}$.\n\n- $3\\sqrt{2}$: It is positive, and it is an irrational real number. Therefore, it does *not* belong to $\\mathbb{Q}_0^{+}$.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is: None of those listed."
