      ":question/id": "e1b54ea3-4e56-5a1d-9c6d-39f851a1214f",
      ":question/problem": "Which of the numbers below belong to $\\mathbb{Q}_0^{+}$?\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Remember that $\\mathbb{Q}_0^{+}$ is the set of all positive rational numbers and zero.\n\n- $-\\sqrt{14}$: It is negative, and it is an irrational real number. Therefore, it does *not* belong to $\\mathbb{Q}_0^{+}$.\n\n- $46$: It is positive, and it is an integer. Therefore, it belongs to $\\mathbb{Q}_0^{+}$.\n\n- $\\frac{\\pi}{2}$: It is positive, and it is an irrational real number. Therefore, it does *not* belong to $\\mathbb{Q}_0^{+}$.\n\n- $\\sqrt{49}=7$: It is positive, and it is an integer. Therefore, it belongs to $\\mathbb{Q}_0^{+}$.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is: $46$; $\\sqrt{49}$."
